76037 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176037 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176037 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Marginal Distribution From a Joint Distribution: Non-Rectangular Domains" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176037 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176037 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bc2759a6-76b0-5410-b695-1e28b3168cb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1045 "Marginal Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176153 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b7c1b63f-a8ec-54b2-8bd2-f993ffba0210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176262 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {4xy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nThe marginal CDF $F_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$F_{X}(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ g(x),\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1; 1 \\mid x > 1$.\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2b8ae16-dd41-57f5-a30f-634ebab68387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176749 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176749 #edb/ref 1061 34136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176749 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ee40682-9346-5226-8f31-7485c414635e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176749 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176755 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176755 #edb/ref 1061 34284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176755 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1c872df-df0e-5ace-95d4-79d04118e90c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176755 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176756 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176756 #edb/ref 1061 34287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176756 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e005bf1f-d37f-59a9-8616-8c44dcccf27f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176756 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176757 #edb/ref 1061 34294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176757 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92b212c1-f8b5-5c20-9ba2-51519f5eb1c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176757 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176838 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176838 #edb/ref 1061 37919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176838 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79e0c173-47c4-57c7-89d0-44529d8a86f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176838 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176843 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176843 #edb/ref 1061 38107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176843 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d45cbe3-5cd1-5dd4-b04c-1c3e4790d561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176843 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176844 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176844 #edb/ref 1061 38110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176844 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9edd5a3-d60c-5e03-ab11-6e9add487f22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176844 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177001 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen how to construct the marginal mass functions for two discrete random variables from their joint probability mass function. We now wish to formulate analogous definitions when our random variables are continuous. \n\nFirst, recall that if $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of the *discrete* random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively, then\n\n- the marginal mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_X(x)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{y \\in S_Y} f(x,y)\n$$\n\n- and the marginal mass function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_Y(y)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S_X} f(x,y).\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are *continuous* random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y).$  \n\n- The **marginal density function** of $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\n- The **marginal density function** of $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f (x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to a marginal density function as a **marginal probability density function** (or **marginal PDF**).\n\nIt's helpful to appreciate the analogy between the discrete and continuous cases as this often helps us to remember their definitions. In summary:\n\n- To convert from discrete to continuous, we turn sums into integrals.\n\n- When we compute $f_X$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $Y.$ In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $y.$\n\n- When we compute $f_Y$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $X.$ In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $x.$\n\nLet's now see a concrete example of how to compute a marginal density function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177001 #edb/ref 1065 5478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177001 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c0ae531f-9bb0-5ba9-bd88-40955c3b086f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177001 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177056 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/e2/e229c78e51036e7fb7b7e9345bbe4f2ede5704032d65d2581b53c8a5f59ef603.svg)\n\nLet's find the expression for the marginal density function $f_X(x)$ of $X.$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\leq x \\leq 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{x}^{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}]-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_X(x)$ is given by\n\n![](images/e8/e8eb5c2dedf51c1b232af9e2dd6a4eb12621daaa78ab34f18a46e26a9a6d2887.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177056 #edb/ref 1065 6292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177056 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3374b507-4cf9-507d-a469-7279c8a65dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177056 #edb/ref 1067 "A Concrete Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177057 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we know the marginal distributions $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ of the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y.$\n\nThen we can define the **marginal cumulative distribution functions** (or **marginal CDFs**) of $X$ and $Y$ analogously as follows:\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X,$ denoted $F_X(x),$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f_X(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y,$ denoted $F_Y(y),$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = P(Y\\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y f_Y(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177057 #edb/ref 1065 6293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177057 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cf432491-2aee-56d2-9488-c13965e4b0c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177057 #edb/ref 1067 "Marginal Cumulative Distribution Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
